format: 2
world: nono
module-attr: nonoModules
schema-pin: LIPS_NIXPKGS_FLAKE

--- preamble ---
TARGET WORLD: nono (https://nono.sh), a capability-based sandbox that runs one
agent, or one tool an agent calls, under a JSON policy. A nono profile
CONFIGURES A SANDBOX, not a machine and not a home: nothing is installed and
no service is wired, so never emit a NixOS or home-manager option here (no
services.*, no systemd.*, no environment.systemPackages, no programs.*). The
namespaces are the profile's own top-level sections, and an option path is a
path into the profile JSON: filesystem.read, network.allow_domain,
command_policies.commands.<tool>.from.session.invocation_policy.default.

<self> is the profile's name: put it in meta.name, which a human then runs as
`nono run --profile <dir>/profile.json -- <agent>`.

WHERE EACH KIND OF PERMISSION BELONGS (distilled from `nono profile guide`,
nono 0.68.0; that guide is the full reference and this is only the part every
program meets):

- Reaching files: filesystem.allow (read+write), filesystem.read (read-only),
  filesystem.write, filesystem.deny, and the single-file forms allow_file,
  read_file, write_file. All are arrays of paths, and this is the enforcement
  that holds against child processes.
- The working directory: workdir.access, one of "none", "read", "write",
  "readwrite".
- Reaching the network: network.allow_domain (array of hostnames, or objects
  carrying endpoint rules), network.deny_domain, network.block (a boolean that
  cannot be combined with proxy settings), network.open_port,
  network.listen_port.
- Running a command: command_policies.commands.<tool>.from.session, which
  carries that tool's own sandbox (fs_read, fs_write, network) and its
  invocation_policy. The invocation_policy has "default" plus the arrays
  "deny", "approve" and "allow", evaluated in that order, whose entries match
  argv AFTER the command name: {"argv": {"prefix": ["push"]}} matches
  `git push`. "approve" is the tier that asks a human before running.
  Two shapes the validator REQUIRES here, measured against nono 0.68.0 and
  0.74.0, because a profile that omits them is refused and neither is
  guessable from the field list:
    * every from.<caller> must carry a sandbox object, even an empty one. An
      entry holding only invocation_policy is refused with "data did not match
      any variant of untagged enum CommandFromConfig".
    * an approve entry needs an approval backend, or the profile is refused
      with missing_approval_backend. Declare one --
      command_policies.approval_backends.<name>.type, one of "terminal",
      "webhook" or "chain" -- and name it in
      command_policies.approval_defaults.backend. "terminal" is the one that
      asks the human sitting at the session.
- Which deny groups apply: groups.include (a profile with no groups.include
  carries no deny rules at all) and groups.exclude.

DO NOT EMIT commands.allow OR commands.deny. That section is deprecated since
nono v0.33.0: it gates only the command that starts the session, is not
enforced on child processes, and a rule written there silently fails to
constrain the agent. Command permission belongs in command_policies.

query_options searches exactly this world's pinned schema, and here it is
WEAK on purpose, as in terranix: the schema carries only the top-level
sections nono's own `nono profile schema` publishes, so the lookup confirms
that a section exists and nothing below it. Treat every deeper field as
unverified: emit only fields you have from the guide above, and refuse rather
than guess a field name. What holds the deeper names to account is nono's own
validator, which `builds` runs over the rendered profile and which rejects an
unknown field by name (49 of the 51 object definitions in its schema are
additionalProperties:false). lips builds that validation before it accepts an
engine, so a field it refuses is a refused mint.

THIS WORLD HOSTS NO CLAIM: there is no machine to boot and no artifact to run,
so state no observable. The profile is gated by `nono profile validate`, which
every render of this module runs.

--- schema ---
# nono ships no Nix module, so the option tree comes from nono's OWN published
# JSON Schema (`nono profile schema`), read out of the nono package in this
# world's pinned nixpkgs -- the same pin every other world grounds against.
#
# Only the TOP-LEVEL sections become options. The schema's 71 nested
# definitions are left unmodelled on purpose, which makes every deeper path
# free-form here (a lookup answers "accepted, and this schema cannot confirm
# it"), exactly as terranix's resource fields are. The deeper names are held to
# account by nono's validator at build time instead.
#
# The jq pass maps JSON Schema types onto the type WORDING a Nix options
# document uses, because that wording is what lips classifies. A section that
# is not a plain scalar stays "JSON value", an unmodelled type, so its fields
# are accepted and left to the validator. The key filter drops "$schema",
# which is a JSON-Schema marker rather than a policy section.
#
# Known gap, stated because it will surprise a reader: nono's parser accepts a
# few top-level names its published schema does not list (policy,
# brokered_commands, binary, skipdirs, platform_overrides). Grounding follows
# the published schema, so a rule writing one of those is refused at mint time
# even though nono would take it. Deduce-or-fail is the right side to err on:
# the remedy is to widen this pass when the schema catches up.
let np = builtins.getFlake "<flakeref>";
    pkgs = import np { system = <system>; };
in pkgs.runCommand "options.json" { } ''
  # HOME and -s are both about what lands on STDOUT: with no home directory
  # and no terminal, nono prints a canonicalize warning and its banner into
  # the redirected stream, and jq then reads a log line as JSON.
  export HOME=$PWD
  ${pkgs.nono}/bin/nono -s profile schema > schema.json
  ${pkgs.jq}/bin/jq '
    .properties
    | with_entries(select(.key | test("^[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*$")))
    | with_entries(.value = { type:
        (if .value.type == "boolean" then "boolean"
         elif .value.type == "string" then "string"
         elif .value.type == "integer" then "signed integer"
         else "JSON value" end) })
  ' schema.json > $out
''

--- builds ---
      # nono has no module system of its own, so the profile is an ordinary
      # free-form module evaluation: every option a rule writes is a key in the
      # profile JSON, and nothing here computes. `_module` is the module
      # system's own bookkeeping, so it is dropped rather than serialized.
      builds = system:
        let
          p = pkgsFor system;
          cfg = (p.lib.evalModules {
            modules = [
              { freeformType = p.lib.types.attrsOf p.lib.types.anything;
                _module.args = { pkgs = p; }; }
              ./default.nix
            ];
          }).config;
          rendered = p.writeText "profile.json"
            (builtins.toJSON (removeAttrs cfg [ "_module" ]));
          # The gate: nono's own validator decides whether the rendered file is
          # a profile. It is the only thing that knows the nested definitions
          # this world's schema deliberately leaves unmodelled, so a field name
          # no rule could ground dies here rather than in a running sandbox.
          profile = p.runCommand "profile.json" { } ''
            # A writable HOME only to keep nono's warning out of the log; the
            # validator itself reads nothing but the file named here.
            export HOME=$PWD
            ${p.nono}/bin/nono profile validate ${rendered}
            cp ${rendered} $out
          '';
        in { inherit profile; };

--- packages ---
        profile = (builds system).profile;

--- gate ---
        # The validated profile IS the verdict: its build runs `nono profile
        # validate`, and lips builds this before accepting an engine, against
        # the same pinned nixpkgs the schema came from. Cheap (one small file,
        # nono from the binary cache), which is why every mint can afford it.
        (builds system).profile

--- apps ---
        let b = builds system; p = pkgsFor system; in {
          profile = { type = "app"; program = "${p.writeShellScript "print-profile" "cat ${b.profile}"}"; };
        }

--- devShells ---
        let p = pkgsFor system; in {
          default = p.mkShell { packages = [ p.nono ]; };
        }

--- rungs ---
write the profile | run | profile | > profile.json
check it validates | build | profile |
a shell with nono | develop | | (nono run --profile ./profile.json -- <agent>)
